    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. so for the live load part, what's the standard psf (pounds per square foot) i should be aiming for with residential decks in bc? also if i'm worried about the beam potentially failing or having some weak points in the middle, how do i factor that in?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For residential decks in British Columbia, the standard live load requirement is typically around 40 pounds per square foot (psf). If you are worried about weak points in the middle of your beams, you're thinking about deflection and shear strength, which is where the structural engineering comes in. You can't just eyeball it; you need to consult the specific BC building codes, which will dictate the required span tables or allow you to calculate the necessary beam size based on the load and the span distance.\n\nIt's a good idea to check the local municipality's bylaws, as some specific projects might have unique requirements, and you might want to hire a local engineer to review your plans before you start cutting wood. They can ensure your design is robust enough to handle the worst-case scenario, like a heavy snow load combined with a group of people enjoying the deck. Remember, these calculations are critical, not just for safety, but also to prevent premature structural failure.",
      "source": "target",
