    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i noticed in some old forum posts people talking about \"unexpected\" failures or structural issues that didn't seem to match the math. like, even when the beams were sized right, something would just go wrong. is there anything else i should watch out for besides just the math? like, are there common hidden factors that cause these kinds of issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those \"unexpected\" failures are often due to factors outside the simple load calculations, which is where the real-world application gets tricky. A common issue is improper connections, like using the wrong type of fasteners or not adequately bracing the joints. You must ensure that the connection points—where the beams meet the posts, or where joists meet the beams—are designed to transfer the load effectively.\n\nAnother hidden factor is wood condition and moisture. If your cedar has been improperly treated or is prone to rot, its actual strength will be far less than the nominal values in the code. You should also pay close attention to how the deck interacts with the ground; poor footing or inadequate drainage can introduce settlement issues that stress the entire structure, even if the beams themselves are perfectly sized. Always check the connection details against the manufacturer's specifications for your hardware.",
      "source": "target",
